# Starship Alexandria project context

Starship Alexandria is a cozy, keyboard-first roguelike about recovering public-domain literature from a post-collapse Earth. It is a desktop web game with no combat, player death, timer, backend, multiplayer, touch controls, or mid-expedition resume.

## Current stack

- Next.js 16 App Router and React 19 provide the application shell and accessible HTML UI.
- Phaser 4.1 renders the 1024×768 spatial world with `FIT` scaling.
- Zustand 5 owns progression, settings, and modal/game-phase state.
- rot.js supports selected deterministic topology builders.
- `public/content/` is the sole narrative-content source.
- Runtime art, fonts, narration, and audio are committed under `public/`; gameplay never downloads remote media.

The authoritative design references are `README.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/CONTENT_AUTHORING.md`, and `docs/THEME_AUTHORING.md`.

## Ownership boundaries

### React

React owns document-like UI: the launch gate, settings, destination picker, dialogue, library/reader, visual and textual maps, focus management, tabs, and DOM/ARIA equivalents for canvas information. Every modal has an explicit Close control, traps and restores focus, and makes the background inert.

### Phaser

Phaser owns rendering, visibility, collision presentation, spatial movement effects, interaction markers, footsteps, and ambience. The active scenes are `BootScene`, `ShipScene`, and `ExploreScene`. Phaser must not parse narrative YAML or decide durable progression.

### Zustand

Zustand is the source of truth for progression, preferences, and modal/game-phase state. Mutate it only through actions. Save schema v6 persists fragment IDs and compact progress/preferences, never excerpt bodies, coordinates, an active map, or expedition clues. Reloads safely recall the player to the ship.

### Typed bridge and input

`src/game/EventBridge.ts` is the only React ↔ Phaser event bus. Add or change a channel in its typed contract and update producers, consumers, and tests together; do not maintain an informal fixed event list elsewhere. React subscriptions must remove the exact listener and remain safe under Strict Mode remounts.

Browser keyboard input is translated to semantic actions by `InputActionRouter`. Native HTML controls are excluded from global game input. Ignore repeated keydown events before routing any action, including movement, so one physical press means one turn.

## Expedition domain

`generateExpedition` in `src/game/expeditions/` is synchronous and must remain free of Phaser, React, Zustand, I/O, wall-clock time, and `Math.random()`. It returns semantic cells, zones, spawn/extraction, entities, and a clue-linked vault.

- Use the named deterministic streams `layout`, `content`, `npc`, `vault`, and `decor`.
- Save and restore rot.js RNG state around every ROT builder.
- Validate at most 20 deterministic candidates, then use the theme-specific fallback.
- Cosmetic/decor changes must not reshuffle layout or gameplay placement.
- Consume `walkable`, `opaque`, `surface`, and `renderRole` as meanings; never infer game rules from atlas frame numbers.

Themes are topology contracts, not palettes:

- `scriptorium`: Digger rooms and corridors.
- `cathedral`: ruined cross-plan, chapels, cloister, and crypt.
- `university`: academic rooms around a connected courtyard loop.
- `gardens`: cellular clearings joined by paths.

### Placement covenant

Fragments, NPCs, and journals may not overlap or sit cardinally adjacent to one another, the spawn, or extraction. NPCs and fragments must occupy disjoint zones. The map, clue, props, and vault still obey their own reachability/collision contracts. Validate the finished output independently of the allocator.

## Interaction behavior

Walkable pickups use on-tile interaction. NPCs and vaults intentionally block their occupied cell and therefore use an adjacent interaction target; keep prompts, collision, BFS tests, and accessible instructions aligned with that representation. The clue is discovered through ordinary interaction and the vault never requires typed code entry.

`GameContainer` handles an interaction before emitting `interactive-consumed`; `ExploreScene` removes consumables only after that success event. This ordering prevents an object disappearing before its React dialogue/progression update succeeds.

## Accessibility and presentation

- Essential information may never exist only through canvas pixels, color, motion, or sound.
- UI follows WCAG 2.2 AA contrast. The game world prioritizes strong floor/wall separation and high-contrast player/interactable silhouettes.
- Retain the player highlight ring and full-brightness collectible markers through fog.
- Wall edges use the shared light outline (`0xe8e4dc`, 4px) without per-tile grid borders.
- ExploreScene depth order is ground 0, wall 1, decoration 2, wall outline 2.5, fog 4, interactives 5, player 10, prompt 100.
- Iterate raw semantic/map arrays for outlines and fog; avoid cross-tilemap `forEachTile`/`getTileAt` calls.
- The textual map reports current location, discovered rooms, approximate directions/distances, and known contents.
- `system | reduce | full` motion settings apply to both CSS and Phaser; reduced-motion paths communicate the same state.
- Audio remains locked on every page load until the launch-gate gesture. Footsteps follow successful movement and the destination cell's semantic surface; ambience pauses while hidden.

## Content and assets

- Edit only `public/content/`; a root `content/` tree is forbidden.
- Preserve the stated Project Gutenberg edition wording and punctuation, strip Gutenberg boilerplate, and retain source/territory metadata.
- Keep runtime media local and provenance-complete. Asset refresh recipes pin source hashes, transformations, licenses, and encoding toolchain requirements.
- Runtime audio requires exactly one OGG and one MP3 per logical sound.
- Keep the visible AI disclosure and model/voice/text-hash manifest for generated opening narration.

## Verification

- Add behavior tests at public seams and use deterministic seeds.
- Generator fuzz coverage runs 250 seeds per theme and checks topology, connectivity, reachability, placement, references, retries, and fallback behavior.
- Playwright derives paths independently with BFS, sends real keyboard input, and does not warp the player or use fixed sleeps.
- `window.__STARSHIP_E2E__` is read-only and build-flagged; it must be absent from production.
- Run `npm run check` and `npm run test:e2e` before showcase release. Visual baseline updates are intentional review actions, never incidental cleanup.

## Do not

- Use Phaser 2/3/CE APIs or reintroduce deleted starter scenes/entities.
- Add another event bus, root content tree, remote runtime URL, or direct store mutation.
- Put critical text or state only on the canvas.
- Introduce paid assets, a backend, multiplayer, combat, mobile controls, or mid-expedition persistence in this refresh.
- Move stable modules or shared types solely to satisfy folder aesthetics; make structural changes when they improve an actual boundary.
